<<hubreturn>>


:: sharma_handwriting_d3
Dr. Sharma pauses at your table. Kind eyes, thick glasses, the paternal warmth that makes you want to confess everything.

<div class="dialogue">"Miss Iyer, your notes today are... different. More organised, actually. New pen?"</div>

Organised because you don't understand the content, so you're copying character by character with the deliberation of a calligrapher, instead of Kavya's rapid shorthand.

"New pen, sir. Trying to be more neat." You angle the notebook away   the angle of someone protecting their work, not hiding evidence.

He nods. Studies you one more second   a second that lasts six years   and moves on. You realise Kavya's knuckles are white around the scalpel.
<<run setup.stat("sharma_suspicion", 1, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: specimen_jar_d3
A crash. Three tables over, a first-year drops a specimen jar. It shatters, formalin-preserved tissue across the stone floor, her face crumbling toward tears.

You're on your feet before you decide to be   not medical instinct, human instinct. You cross the tables, crouch, help the attendant collect the largest glass.

<div class="dialogue">"Theek hai. Hota hai. Pehle saal mein sabke saath hota hai."<span class="gloss-en">It's okay. It happens. It happens to everyone in first year.</span></div>

You say it in Kavya's voice, gentler than you expected. She sniffles. Nods.

Dr. Sharma watches from across the room, expression complicated. Three years of Kavya's technical precision, and he's never seen her comfort a student with warmth.

<div class="dialogue">"Miss Iyer. That was kind. You showed the human side of medicine today. I'm proud of you."</div>

Later, in the corridor, Meera catches your arm.

<div class="dialogue">"That was //you//. Kavya would've kept dissecting. Different is risk."</div>

She's right. But the first-year stopped crying.
<<run setup.flag("specimen_helped_d3")>>\
<<run setup.stat("med_knowledge", 3, "arjun")>>\
<<run setup.stat("rel_kavya_meera", 0, "kavya")>>\
